        (52, 53, """If an overflow happened during that loop, samples were lost, so the stored waveform has a hole in it and the old history would give a wrong period. Calling `heart.reset()` throws the history away and starts again, at the cost of a few seconds before the next reading."""),
        (55, 55, """The millisecond clock, read once and used by the two slow jobs below."""),
        (57, 58, """Is a Serial line due, and are we *not* in raw-capture mode? During a `p` capture the normal line is suppressed so that only clean CSV reaches the file."""),
        (59, 59, """Schedules the next line 100 ms from now."""),
        (60, 60, """Prints the three gyro rates (degrees per second, one decimal) and the latest raw infrared count. `%.1f` is a float with one decimal; `%lu` is an unsigned long, which is why `ppg.ir` is cast with `(unsigned long)`. No `\\n` yet: the line continues below. The gyro values are raw sensor output with no zero-offset removal, so they read a few degrees per second even when the board is still (in testing, roughly between -4 and +3)."""),
        (61, 61, """Appends ` hr=84` only when the heart rate is currently valid. When it is not, the field is simply absent rather than showing a stale or zero value."""),
        (62, 63, """Appends the step total and ends the line with `\\n`. A finished line looks like `gx:2.3,gy:-2.5,gz:-0.8,pulse:165523  hr=84  steps=14`. The `label:value,label:value` format is also what Arduino's Serial Plotter understands, so the same output can be graphed."""),
        (65, 66, """Is a Bluetooth packet due?"""),
        (67, 67, """Schedules the next one 500 ms from now. Unlike the IMU, this uses `now + period`, so it can drift by a few milliseconds per packet. That is irrelevant for a 2 Hz status update."""),
        (68, 70, """Builds and sends the 20-byte packet. `steps.count` is the step total; `(uint16_t)steps.cadence` turns the float cadence into a whole number by cutting off the decimals; `steps.walking` says walking or stationary; `(uint8_t)(heart.bpm + 0.5f)` rounds the heart rate to the nearest whole number (adding 0.5 before cutting off decimals is the usual rounding trick); `heart.valid` and `heart.contact` are the two flags; `imuOk` and `ppgOk` are the boot results. The call is made every 500 ms even when no phone is connected; the Bluetooth module decides whether to actually transmit."""),
